WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.389 --> 00:00:20.620
This paramecium is not having a particularly
good day.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:27.340
You wouldn’t be either if the contents of
your body were being slowly consumed by a ciliate.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:35.320
But as you, hopefully, are watching this from
a comfortable position, while not being consumed,

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:41.000
we would understand if your attention is diverted
from the microbial drama and instead focused

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:49.649
on all the shiny things, like looking down
on a city at night from an airplane, the moving

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:54.120
lights a sign of life at a distance.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:59.680
These shiny colors are actually crystals,
visible this way under polarized light.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.540
So, we have to use a special filter to get
these shots and they seem to be connected

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:07.360
to the paramecium’s metabolism.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:12.280
We wanted to start with these crystals because,
like, we think they’re stunning.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:18.479
And stunning is not necessarily the first
word that comes to mind when you think of paramecia.

00:01:18.479 --> 00:01:23.930
Of the early microorganisms discovered by
scientists, paramecia have gone on to have

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:27.630
perhaps the most distinguished career.

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:32.550
Studied by scientists and students alike,
these protozoans have been so useful as a

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:37.750
model organism that one scientist called them
the “white rat” of ciliates.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:43.210
But while paramecia have played an important
role in our understanding of genetics, microscopic

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:51.100
movement, and more, importance does not always
imply excitement or enthusiasm.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:56.840
After all, if we compare paramecia to their
protozoan brethren, what does a hairy oval

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:03.079
have next to the shifting shapes of the amoeba
or the strange movements of the euglenoid?

00:02:03.079 --> 00:02:08.570
That doesn’t stop paramecia from wanting
some attention though: there have been plenty

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:14.060
of times where we’ve set up a recording
of some beautiful organism, only to have paramecia

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:18.730
come by and photobomb our precious composition.

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:25.390
Their reliability in labs and frequent appearance
in classrooms might make paramecia seem like

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:27.370
a known quantity.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.300
The things we know, we seem to have a good
grasp on.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:36.660
We know that this is a paramecium, a unicellular
eukaryote covered in tiny hairs called cilia

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:38.470
that help it swim.

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:43.800
We know that when scientists first began describing
paramecia, they divided them into two groups

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:51.120
based on their morphology: Paramecium aurelia,
which have a long, tapered oval shape; and

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:54.690
Paramecium bursaria, which are shorter and
rounder.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:03:00.420
We also know that they have multiple nuclei:
one macronucleus where transcription happens,

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:07.090
and a number of micronuclei that vary across species that become important during sexual reproduction.

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:12.580
We’ve talked about p aramecia in a previous
episode, describing the endosymbiotic algae

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:19.400
that reside inside of some species, providing
sugar produced through photosynthesis in exchange

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.940
for the movement and shelter of the surrounding
cell.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.720
So much of their knownness rests on just how
widespread they are.

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:35.450
You can find paramecia in freshwater, brackish,
and marine environments around the world,

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.690
though the distribution, of course, varies
by species.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:45.700
The ones that we’ve come to know best seem
to be the ones that are considered cosmopolitan,

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:50.200
that’s a word we use for things that are
found seemingly everywhere.

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:58.180
Recently, scientists studying Paramecium biaurelia
collected from 92 spots around the world over

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:05.380
62 years of time found very little genetic
variation between the different strains.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:10.629
This kind of genetic similarity indicates
not only that these organisms have travelled

00:04:10.629 --> 00:04:17.880
the world, but that they continue to, keeping
their populations spread out and not diversifying

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:20.110
into different species.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:28.550
But the ubiquity that makes paramecia so known
to us is itself a question: how did paramecia

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:34.339
manage to find their way to all these different
locations, especially bodies of water that

00:04:34.339 --> 00:04:36.920
are isolated from others?

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.690
Unlike some of the other aquatic organisms
we’ve discussed on this channel, paramecium

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:47.440
don’t form protective cysts that keep them
safe during a dry spell, which makes it that

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:52.380
much harder for them to cross the globe and
its many dry spots.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:58.330
Through experimentation and observation, scientists
have confirmed the presence of hitch-hiking

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:04.810
paramecia on the wet fur of raccoons and on
the bodies of snails, showing that they might

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:12.330
be using the surface of animals as moist taxis,
but whether that explains the entirety of

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.710
their travels remains to be seen.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:20.029
Paramecia are also subject to what you might
start to notice is a recurring theme when

00:05:20.029 --> 00:05:22.380
it comes to the microcosmos.

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:28.729
While our early classification methods relied
on what we could see of an organism’s morphology,

00:05:28.729 --> 00:05:34.760
the development of various molecular techniques
is uncovering new patterns and complications

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:41.020
in how we define the relationships between
its species.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:46.670
These complications were hinted at by early
observations, like the fact that the broad,

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:52.620
morphologically-defined label of P. aurelia
couldn’t capture the strange mating subdivisions

00:05:52.620 --> 00:06:00.169
that occured in the species, groups of P.
aurelia seemed reproductively isolated from each other.

00:06:00.169 --> 00:06:07.520
In time, as scientists gathered more observations, P. aurelia stopped referring to one species of paramecia.

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:14.710
Instead, it was redefined as a complex of
fourteen siblings named P. primaurelia, P.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:21.089
biaurelia, P. triaurelia…all the way up
to P. quadecaurelia.

00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:27.570
And a fifteenth subspecies named P. sonneborni
was discovered later.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:32.779
With more observation and genetic techniques,
the Paramecium genus has come to encompass

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:41.300
19 morphospecies, comprised of what are sometimes
called “cryptic species” these are species

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:46.140
who morphological similarities belie their
underlying differences.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:52.800
When we look at them in the microscope, there
is no way for us to tell which species it is.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:55.840
We would need to sequence their genome.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:01.649
With words like “cosmopolitan” and “cryptic”
used as technical terms to describe paramecia,

00:07:01.649 --> 00:07:09.570
they start to seem less like a textbook organism
and more like an international microbe of mystery.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:17.200
It might be fitting then to learn from the
experience of the scientist Tracy M. Sonneborn,

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:21.390
who began working with protozoa in the 1930s.

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:27.330
Sonneborn developed many of the techniques
that turned paramecia into a model organism,

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:34.690
his work so influential that it has been memorialized
in the name of that 15th P. aurelia subspecies,

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:38.020
P. sonneborni.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:44.520
While researching paramecia, Sonneborn became
interested in the so-called killer trait,

00:07:44.520 --> 00:07:53.260
a subset of paramecia that seemed able to
produce toxins that killed other paramecia.

00:07:53.260 --> 00:08:01.570
These killer paramecia all shared a trait:
the presence of mysterious cytoplasmic particles

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:04.280
called kappa.

00:08:04.280 --> 00:08:10.940
Sonneborn tracked how these kappa particles
passed on through generations, proposing the

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:17.620
“plasmagene theory” to explain the patterns
he saw, a hypothesis that these kappa particles

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:26.390
were some special thing coded in the paramecium’s
DNA, but that could self-replicate on their

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:28.760
own in the cytoplasm.

00:08:28.760 --> 00:08:31.460
And he was wrong.

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:39.190
It turned out that these kappa particles aren’t
particles, they are bacteria: another example

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:46.050
of endosymbiosis, except that instead of providing
the nourishing promise of photosynthesis,

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:52.020
these bacteria turned their paramecium homes
into killers.

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:07.400
It took time for Sonneborn to accept that
his plasmagene theory didn’t explain kappa particles.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:12.640
But if paramecia have come to model so much
of what we learn in science, then looking

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:21.380
more broadly, this microscopic organism also
models us and our own use of the scientific process.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:26.860
In a letter to another researcher, Sonneborn
reflected on this, on what it means to set

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:36.460
aside his own ego for the reality of the microbe,
to know that studying nature requires an understanding

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:38.270
of our own selves.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:46.020
He wrote, “‘It was awful of me to be so
attached to a pet idea.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:51.720
That was an ordeal between my mind and my
heart and it took a while for the mind to

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.710
win and the heart to accept.

00:09:54.710 --> 00:10:01.690
Impersonal scientific objectivity is a goal
to be sought by hard self-discipline; we are

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:03.500
not born with it.’’

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:15.260
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:18.110
Journey to the Microcosmos is produced by
Complexly.

00:10:18.110 --> 00:10:24.810
We produce over a dozen different shows, including
a new one, Ours Poetica, it’s a co-production

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:29.800
between Complexly, The Poetry Foundation,
and the poet Paige Lewis.

00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:36.800
Ours Poetica brings you a new poem three times
a week, read by poets, writers, artists, and

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:42.010
sometimes unexpected, yet familiar, voices
like... like mine for example.

00:10:42.010 --> 00:10:46.890
You can check out a video of me reading Edgar
Alan Poe's "The Raven".

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:52.950
We think there are times for YouTube to be
high energy and times for it to be a little

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:54.840
bit more relaxing.

00:10:54.840 --> 00:11:00.540
Journey to the Microcosmos and Ours Poetica
are both going for that second thing.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:04.870
I also want to say thank you for listening
to my extra gravely voice today.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:06.720
I have a cold.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:09.300
We haven’t talked much about viruses on
this channel.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:14.480
They are very difficult to observe, they’re
tiny, probably aren’t really even alive.

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:21.780
But they have colonized much of my upper respiratory
system right now and I’m just pushing through.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:26.190
Also thank you so much to everyone who has
signed up to be one of our patrons on Patreon.

00:11:26.190 --> 00:11:30.690
We are extremely grateful to be able to make
this content and you are the reason why we

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:31.790
get to.

00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:37.070
And if you want to see more from our Master
of Microscopes James, check out Jam and Germs

00:11:37.070 --> 00:11:38.000
on Instagram.

